    content: `"The hypothalamic–pituitary axis" is not a single pathway but a shared design used by several hormone systems, each a three-level cascade with feedback. LEVEL 1: the HYPOTHALAMUS secretes a tiny amount of a "releasing hormone" (or an inhibiting hormone) into a special short loop of blood vessels — the hypophyseal PORTAL system — that carries it a few millimetres directly to the anterior pituitary. LEVEL 2: the ANTERIOR PITUITARY responds by secreting a "tropic" hormone into the general bloodstream. LEVEL 3: the tropic hormone reaches a peripheral TARGET GLAND and makes it release its own hormone, which then acts on the body AND feeds back to SUPPRESS both the hypothalamus and the pituitary — negative feedback that holds the final hormone within a set range, like a thermostat. THE MAIN AXES: (1) HYPOTHALAMIC–PITUITARY–THYROID — TRH → TSH → thyroxine (T4) and T3. (2) HYPOTHALAMIC–PITUITARY–ADRENAL (HPA) — CRH → ACTH → cortisol; this is the body's stress axis and also runs a daily rhythm. (3) HYPOTHALAMIC–PITUITARY–GONADAL — GnRH (in pulses) → LH and FSH → oestrogen/progesterone (ovary) or testosterone (testis). (4) HYPOTHALAMIC–PITUITARY–SOMATOTROPIC — GHRH (stimulates) and somatostatin (inhibits) → growth hormone → the liver makes IGF-1. Prolactin is the odd one out: the hypothalamus mostly INHIBITS it, via dopamine. The posterior pituitary is different again — it isn't a gland but a storage-and-release terminal for ADH and oxytocin made by hypothalamic neurons. The feedback design is clinically powerful: to find out if a target gland is over- or under-functioning, you measure the tropic hormone. A HIGH TSH means the thyroid is failing (the pituitary is compensating by shouting louder); a suppressed LOW TSH means the thyroid is overactive.`,
