    content: `The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland sitting low at the front of the neck, wrapped around the trachea just below the voice box. Its principal and defining function is to make the THYROID HORMONES: thyroxine (T4, with four iodine atoms) and the more potent triiodothyronine (T3, three iodine atoms), which it builds by attaching IODINE (trapped from the blood) to the amino acid tyrosine within a large protein called thyroglobulin, then releasing them into the circulation (mostly as T4, which the tissues convert to active T3). Thyroid hormone acts on nuclear receptors in virtually every cell of the body and sets the "idle speed" of metabolism — the basal metabolic rate. Its effects: it increases oxygen consumption and heat production (so hyperthyroidism causes heat intolerance and hypothyroidism cold intolerance), speeds up the heart rate and increases its contractile force, increases gut motility, affects the metabolism of carbohydrate, fat, and protein, is essential for normal growth and — critically — for brain development in the fetus and infant (untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes intellectual disability, "cretinism," which is why newborns are screened), and influences mood, energy, skin, hair, and menstrual regularity. Thyroid hormone production is governed by TSH from the anterior pituitary, which in turn is driven by TRH from the hypothalamus, in a negative-feedback loop (high T4 shuts off TSH). A distinct minority population of thyroid cells, the parafollicular "C cells," secretes CALCITONIN, a hormone that lowers blood calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts — but in humans this plays only a minor role in calcium balance (PTH and vitamin D do the heavy lifting), and its main clinical importance is as a tumour marker for medullary thyroid cancer.`,
