    content: `HbA1c (glycated or glycosylated haemoglobin, "A1c") is a blood test that measures the proportion of the haemoglobin molecules inside your red blood cells that have had a glucose molecule spontaneously and permanently attached to them ("glycation"). The higher your average blood glucose, the more haemoglobin gets glycated. Because a red blood cell lives about 120 days and cannot remove the glucose once it's stuck, the HbA1c value reflects the AVERAGE blood glucose over roughly the previous 8–12 weeks (weighted toward the most recent 4 weeks, which contribute ~50%). This makes it fundamentally different from a fingerstick glucose, which is a single point-in-time reading: HbA1c cannot be fooled by fasting for the appointment, and it captures the overall glucose exposure that actually drives long-term complications. It is reported as a percentage (older units) or in mmol/mol (IFCC units). DIAGNOSTIC CUT-OFFS (WHO/ADA): NORMAL — below 5.7% (below 39 mmol/mol); PRE-DIABETES (impaired glucose regulation, raised future risk) — 5.7% to 6.4% (39–47 mmol/mol); DIABETES — 6.5% or higher (48 mmol/mol or higher), confirmed on a repeat test in the absence of symptoms. It is a common mistake to say "anything over 5.7% is diabetes" — 5.7–6.4% is only pre-diabetes. For someone already diagnosed with diabetes, a typical management TARGET is below 7% (below 53 mmol/mol), individualised (tighter in young/newly diagnosed, looser in the frail elderly or those with hypoglycaemia). LIMITATIONS: HbA1c is inaccurate — and should not be used to diagnose diabetes — when the red-cell lifespan or haemoglobin is abnormal: anaemia (iron-deficiency raises it, haemolytic anaemia lowers it), recent major blood loss or transfusion, chronic kidney or liver disease, pregnancy, and haemoglobinopathies (sickle cell trait, thalassaemia); it also lags behind a rapidly changing glucose (e.g. new type 1 diabetes), where a plasma glucose or continuous monitor is used instead.`,
