    content: `Hormones fall into two broad chemical classes that act by completely different mechanisms. STEROID HORMONES — cortisol, aldosterone, oestrogen, progesterone, testosterone, and (although not steroids, they behave the same way) thyroid hormone and calcitriol (active vitamin D). Properties: they are small and LIPID-soluble (fat-soluble), synthesised from cholesterol, NOT stored in the gland (made on demand and released immediately), and — because they don't dissolve well in blood — carried mostly bound to carrier proteins (albumin, sex-hormone-binding globulin, corticosteroid-binding globulin), with only the small "free" fraction active. MECHANISM: being fat-soluble, a steroid hormone diffuses straight THROUGH the target cell's plasma membrane and binds an INTRACELLULAR receptor in the cytoplasm or nucleus; the hormone–receptor complex then binds specific DNA sequences and acts as a transcription factor, switching particular genes ON or OFF, changing which proteins the cell makes. Consequences: the response is SLOW to appear (hours to days, because it depends on new protein synthesis) but LONG-LASTING, and steroid drugs can usually be taken by mouth (they survive digestion and cross the gut wall). PEPTIDE AND PROTEIN HORMONES — insulin, glucagon, growth hormone, ADH, oxytocin, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH, PTH, calcitonin, prolactin, leptin, and most gut hormones; the catecholamines (adrenaline) are amines derived from tyrosine but act like peptides. Properties: they are WATER-soluble, synthesised as larger precursors and STORED in secretory vesicles, released by exocytosis in bursts, and travel free (unbound) in plasma with a short half-life (minutes). MECHANISM: being water-soluble, they CANNOT cross the lipid membrane, so they bind a receptor on the OUTSIDE of the cell (a G-protein-coupled receptor or an enzyme-linked receptor); this activates intracellular "second messenger" systems (cyclic AMP, IP3/calcium, kinase cascades) that rapidly modify enzymes already present in the cell. Consequences: the response is FAST (seconds to minutes) but SHORT-LIVED, and these hormones must be INJECTED because the digestive tract would break them down (which is why insulin is a needle, not a pill).`,
