    content: `Gynaecomastia is the benign proliferation of true GLANDULAR breast tissue in a male, felt as a firm, rubbery, often tender disc of tissue concentric with the nipple. It results from an imbalance between OESTROGEN action (which stimulates breast tissue) and ANDROGEN action (which opposes it) — either too much oestrogen, too little androgen, or increased conversion of androgen to oestrogen. It must be distinguished from two things: (1) PSEUDOGYNAECOMASTIA / adipomastia — simple fatty enlargement of the chest with no glandular disc, common in obesity; and (2) MALE BREAST CANCER — a hard, irregular, fixed, usually painless, eccentric (off-centre) lump, often with skin dimpling or nipple retraction or discharge, which needs urgent assessment. CAUSES of true gynaecomastia: PHYSIOLOGICAL — (a) newborn boys, from transplacental maternal oestrogen (resolves in weeks); (b) puberty, where a transient surge of oestrogen relative to testosterone gives a tender bump in up to half of boys, usually resolving within 1–2 years; (c) older men, from an age-related fall in testosterone plus increased aromatase activity in expanding body fat. PATHOLOGICAL — hypogonadism (Klinefelter syndrome — 47,XXY, testicular injury, mumps orchitis, undescended testes), chronic LIVER disease/cirrhosis (impaired oestrogen clearance, low SHBG, alcohol), chronic KIDNEY disease and dialysis, HYPERthyroidism, malnutrition and "refeeding" after starvation, and hormone-secreting TUMOURS — germ-cell/Leydig-cell testicular tumours, hCG-secreting cancers (lung, testis), and adrenal or pituitary tumours. DRUGS are a very common cause: anti-androgens (spironolactone, cyproterone, bicalutamide, finasteride), cimetidine and proton-pump inhibitors, some antipsychotics and antidepressants (via raised prolactin), calcium-channel blockers, digoxin, ketoconazole, HIV antiretrovirals, chemotherapy, exogenous oestrogens or their withdrawal, ANABOLIC STEROIDS (the aromatised excess androgen becomes oestrogen, and gynaecomastia flares on withdrawal), and recreational drugs — cannabis, opioids, amphetamines, and alcohol. Assessment looks for medications, liver/kidney/thyroid disease, testicular examination, and blood tests (testosterone, LH, FSH, oestradiol, hCG, prolactin, TSH, LFTs). Most cases (especially pubertal and drug-related) resolve when the cause is removed; persistent, distressing, or long-standing gynaecomastia may need medication (tamoxifen) early or surgery later.`,
