    keywords: [
      'what is the menstrual cycle hormonally follicular ovulation luteal fsh lh oestrogen progesterone',
      'the roughly 28 day cycle is driven by a feedback loop between the hypothalamus pituitary and ovaries and has three phases',
      'follicular phase days 1 to 13 fsh from the pituitary stimulates several ovarian follicles to grow they secrete rising oestrogen which rebuilds proliferates the uterine lining and at first suppresses fsh and lh ovulation about day 14 oestrogen from the dominant follicle peaks and flips to positive feedback triggering a surge of lh which ruptures the follicle and releases the egg',
      'luteal phase days 15 to 28 the emptied follicle becomes the corpus luteum which secretes progesterone plus some oestrogen making the lining secretory and receptive and suppressing gnrh lh fsh if there is no pregnancy the corpus luteum dies after about 10 to 12 days progesterone and oestrogen crash the lining is shed as the period and the drop releases fsh to start again if there is a pregnancy hcg from the embryo keeps the corpus luteum alive',
    ],
    content: `The menstrual cycle (average 28 days, normal range ~21–35) is orchestrated by a feedback loop between the hypothalamus (GnRH), the anterior pituitary (FSH and LH), and the ovaries (oestrogen and progesterone), and it runs two linked cycles at once — the OVARIAN cycle (follicle → ovulation → corpus luteum) and the UTERINE/ENDOMETRIAL cycle (menstruation → proliferation → secretion). Day 1 is the first day of bleeding. FOLLICULAR PHASE (roughly days 1–13): at the start, oestrogen and progesterone are low, which releases the pituitary to secrete FSH; FSH recruits a cohort of ovarian follicles to grow, and their granulosa cells secrete increasing OESTROGEN. Rising oestrogen does two things: it stimulates the endometrium to thicken and regrow ("proliferative phase"), and — while it is at low-to-moderate levels — it exerts NEGATIVE feedback that lowers FSH, so only the single most sensitive ("dominant") follicle survives while the rest regress. OVULATION (around day 14): once the dominant follicle's oestrogen output climbs above a threshold and stays high for ~2 days, the feedback SWITCHES to POSITIVE — high oestrogen now triggers a large, brief SURGE of LH (and a smaller FSH surge) from the pituitary. The LH surge causes the follicle to complete meiosis, swell, and rupture ~36 hours later, releasing the egg (oocyte) into the fallopian tube. LUTEAL PHASE (days 15–28, and reliably ~14 days long): the collapsed follicle transforms into the CORPUS LUTEUM ("yellow body"), which secretes large amounts of PROGESTERONE and some oestrogen. Progesterone converts the endometrium to a "secretory," glycogen-rich, blood-vessel-rich state ready to receive an embryo, raises basal body temperature by ~0.3–0.5 °C, thickens the cervical mucus, and strongly suppresses GnRH/LH/FSH so no new follicle develops. IF NO PREGNANCY: the corpus luteum has a fixed lifespan of ~10–12 days and then degenerates ("luteolysis"); progesterone and oestrogen levels plummet; the deprived endometrium's blood vessels constrict and the lining sloughs off — MENSTRUATION; and the falling hormones release the pituitary to raise FSH and begin the next cycle. IF PREGNANCY OCCURS: the implanting embryo secretes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG — what a pregnancy test detects), which "rescues" the corpus luteum and keeps it making progesterone until the placenta takes over at ~8–10 weeks. Hormonal contraception works by adding synthetic oestrogen and/or progestogen that suppress the FSH/LH surge (blocking ovulation), thin the endometrium, and thicken cervical mucus.`,
