    id: 'kb-gap-endo-signalling',
    title: 'Endocrine, Paracrine, and Autocrine Signalling',
    category: 'Physiology',
    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between an endocrine paracrine and autocrine signal',
      'these describe how far a chemical signal travels from the cell that released it endocrine the signal a hormone is secreted into the bloodstream and carried throughout the body acting on any distant cell that carries the matching receptor insulin cortisol thyroid hormone',
      'paracrine the signal is released into the tissue fluid and diffuses only a short distance to act on nearby neighbouring cells histamine in an allergic reaction nitric oxide relaxing the muscle of a nearby blood vessel many growth factors and gut hormones',
      'autocrine the signal acts back on the very same cell that secreted it some immune cytokines and many cancer cells which secrete their own growth factors related terms juxtacrine signalling through direct membrane to membrane contact and synaptic or neurocrine a neuron releasing a transmitter across a tiny gap onto an immediately adjacent target',
    ],
    content: `Cell-to-cell chemical signals are classified by the DISTANCE they act over and how they are delivered. ENDOCRINE signalling: a cell (usually in a ductless "endocrine gland") secretes a HORMONE directly into the bloodstream, which carries it throughout the entire body; it then acts only on "target" cells anywhere that happen to carry the specific receptor for it. Because it is diluted in the whole blood volume, endocrine signalling is relatively slow to start, low in concentration, and long-lasting, and it coordinates whole-body processes — examples: insulin (from the pancreas, acting on liver, muscle, fat all over the body), cortisol, thyroid hormone, oestrogen, ADH. PARACRINE signalling: a cell releases a signalling molecule into the surrounding interstitial (tissue) fluid, where it diffuses only a SHORT distance and acts on NEIGHBOURING cells of a different type — it does not enter the general circulation (or is broken down or taken up before it can). Examples: histamine released by mast cells acting on nearby vessels and nerves in an allergic/inflammatory reaction; nitric oxide released by vascular endothelium relaxing the smooth muscle immediately beneath it; many growth factors, cytokines, prostaglandins, and gut hormones (somatostatin from delta cells suppressing the adjacent insulin- and glucagon-secreting cells). AUTOCRINE signalling: a cell releases a signal that binds receptors on ITSELF (or on identical adjacent cells), reinforcing or modulating its own behaviour. Examples: interleukin-2 secreted by an activated T lymphocyte driving its own proliferation; and, importantly in disease, many cancer cells that secrete their own growth factors to stimulate their own unchecked division. RELATED MODES: JUXTACRINE (contact-dependent) signalling requires the two cells to physically touch, with a membrane-bound ligand on one binding a receptor on the other (Notch signalling, immune-cell interactions). SYNAPTIC / NEUROCRINE signalling is a neuron releasing a neurotransmitter across a very narrow (~20 nm) synaptic cleft onto a precisely targeted adjacent cell — fast, brief, and specific. NEUROENDOCRINE cells (like the hypothalamic neurons that make ADH and oxytocin) blur the line: they are neurons that release their product into the blood as a hormone.`,
